 | 0268 | 2026-11-21 | Alloys phase P7c: **Pure Tillium is switched on** (`PURE_TILLIUM_READY` true), so Connections offers it for Catalogue, Stock, Discounts, Customers, Orders and Gift cards, and a shop can run on it with no Shopify account. What it took: (1) **Shared state is now optional** (a design change from the plan, which had Pure Tillium supply a one-device Shared state): Off means each register works alone, which is exactly a one-iPad Pure Tillium shop; Shopify and iCloud shops are unaffected because today's setup always sets it. The required Elements are now Catalogue and Orders. (2) **A built-in location**: stock, the half-hourly catalogue refresh and several screens only work when `settings.locationId` is set, and Pure Tillium has no locations, so saving a choice whose catalogue connection has none sets `locationId` to `local` (name "This iPad") and sets any real Shopify location aside in `settings.priorLocation`; going back (Go back to the current setup, or choosing Shopify) restores it exactly. `local` is never sent to other registers (outgoing skip, incoming ignore), which may be on Shopify. New optional `Alloy.needsLocation` (Shopify true). (3) After switching, the next poll reloads the catalogue from the new connection (the location differs, so the refresh is due). (4) **Setup** checklist step 1 reads "Choose where the shop's data lives" with a link to Connections when the connection is not Shopify, and is done without a Shopify login; Shopify's wording is unchanged. `Refresh stock from <name>` on the Inventory screen names the connection. (5) Saving a choice that moves an Element to a different connection now warns, in plain words, that what is in the old one is **not** copied (the Smelter, P8, will do that). (6) The picker shows Pure Tillium's limit: held on this iPad only, not shared with other registers. Not done, on purpose: sharing Pure Tillium data between registers; the QR setup copies settings but not Pure Tillium data; reports and the assistant still say Shopify in places. In a node run AsyncStorage loads its web build and cannot save, so tests use `localStoreUseMemory()`. Tests: `tests/alloyp7c.test.ts` (5); three existing tests updated (registry list, picker options, required list). Device test T121. | `src/alloys/index.ts`, `src/alloyhost/{choice,names,persistentStore}.ts`, `src/alloykit/{elements,types}.ts`, `src/lib/{setupFlow,sharedSettings}.ts`, `src/screens/{Connections,Setup,Inventory}.tsx`, `src/state/store.ts`, tests |
 | 0269 | 2026-11-22 | Alloys phase P8a: **the Smelter's plan and dry run**. `src/alloykit/smelter.ts` (pure `buildPlan`) says what copying a shop from one connection to another would do: items matched by SKU, then barcode, then name and price; archived and draft items left behind; options become their own named items; stock carried with the item; collections matched by title; customers matched by email, phone, then name; and an always-shown list of what is not moved (order history, gift cards, discounts, pictures). `src/alloyhost/smelter.ts` reads both Alloys directly and never writes. Catalogue gained an optional `createCollection`, built in Pure Tillium. Nothing runs yet (P8b). Device test: none, no screen yet. |
 | 0270 | 2026-11-22 | Alloys phase P8b: **the Smelter runs, and Connections has Move my data**. `runPlan` does what the dry run showed, in the destination only: items with stock, then collections (members found by SKU, barcode or name, so a resumed run still fills them), then customers. Refused in practice mode and when the destination is not set up; one bad row is listed and the rest carry on; five failures in a row stop it. Safe to run again, because the plan is rebuilt from what the destination now holds. The screen previews first and copies only after confirming. Device test: T122. |
+| 0271 | 2026-11-22 | Alloys phase P9a: **the old-path shims are gone**. The eleven one-line files under `src/lib/shopify/` and the Zeller one are deleted and every import now points at the real place: the card-reader result code is back at `src/lib/zeller.ts` as the app's own (it is shared by every reader), the gift-card once-only helpers moved to `src/alloykit/giftOnce.ts`, the Shopify secrets are imported from `src/lib/secrets.ts`, and the Shopify sign-in and error helpers the screens still need are re-exported by the registry (`src/alloys/index.ts`), the one file allowed to import an Alloy. `TRANSITION.shims` is empty and the Zeller app-import exceptions are gone; only the Shopify, Zeller bridge and iCloud app imports remain (P9b). Help pages and `help.json` name the new paths. No behaviour change. Device test: none needed, but T10 (sign-in) is the one to repeat if you want a check. |
 | 0241 | 2026-11-14 | **B7e, Help pictures for three more pages (your request).** The picture engine (`src/lib/helpFigures.ts`) already drew 12 pages; this adds seven figures drawn from the real screens' labels: Cloud sync (set up, invite link, join, Back up everything and Export all events, which the page did not describe at all), Offline and sync errors (the Support & diagnostics Sync rows, with the new **Last full sync** line explained) and Split and part payments (the Split amount sheet and the Payment screen after a split). 15 of 68 pages now have a figure. No code; `help.json` rebuilt, the 63 help tests pass. Not looked at on a device: the pictures follow the screens' labels but I cannot see the iPad. The other 53 pages are not drawn; the Daily float report is the hardest (it draws notes and coins) and was left. | B7e |
 | 0240 | 2026-11-14 | **B8, review of the gift-card calls before the live test (your request).** Read `giftcards.ts` against Shopify's `giftCardCredit` and `giftCardDebit` pages: the mutations, fields and the `write_gift_card_transactions` scope are as documented. Shopify gives neither call an idempotency key, and the cancel side already looked in the card's history before crediting again, but the **charge did not**: if Shopify took the charge and the reply was lost, the app said "could not be charged", the cashier tapped again and the card was charged twice. New `debitGiftCardOnce` and `src/lib/shopify/giftCardOnce.ts`: the Nth payment of a sale has the same note on every retry (`POS sale <8 chars> tender N`), the card's history is read for it first, and a debit already there is not sent again (Shopify returns a debit as a negative amount, so amounts are compared by size). The credit side now uses the same helper. Pay.tsx charges through it. `tests/giftcardonce.test.ts` (6 tests) pass. DEVICE_TESTS T10 gains the Wi-Fi-off charge test and a check of the notes. **The live run is still yours:** it spends real gift-card value on your store. | B8 |
 | 0239 | 2026-11-11 | **iCloud was slow, and polls could pile up.** Found by reading the code, not measured on a device: the 25 second tick called `pollShared()` straight away with no check for one already running, and the change pushes and the Sync now button call it too. One poll is about twenty iCloud round trips in a row, so on a slow connection it takes longer than 25 seconds and every tick started another, all competing for iCloud and rewriting the same local state; a cash drawer closed on one iPad then took minutes to show on the other. `pollShared` is now single-flight (`src/lib/singleFlight.ts`): a call made while a poll runs joins it. Diagnostics ▸ Sync gains a **Last full sync** line with the total time and the slowest parts (`src/lib/pollTiming.ts`), so the next step is based on real numbers. Not changed, and likely still slow: the poll's steps run one after another, Back up everything and the events export read 100 records per round trip, and the iCloud tab asks iCloud four things in a row to connect. Parallelising the steps is the next lever but needs a look at what each writes first. Not run on a device. `tests/companionjobs`, `logosync` and `noticesync` cannot run in the sandbox (missing modules) and fail the same without this change; their source checks were run by hand and hold. | Sync |
